"For those who have used it, is the Huckleberry app premium worth paying for? (https://www.reddit.com/r/NewParents/comments/1fwzsfj/for_those_who_have_used_it_is_the_huckleberry_app/)\nciteturn73reddit12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; \n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+39 votes]\nMy wife and I just recently downloaded this app today. It's way easier for us to keep track of his diaper changes, what was in those diapers, feeding times/amounts, etc. Our little bundle of joy is 6 days old. If anyone on here has this app, do you have the premium version? Is it worth it, or is it a cash grab? The premium is supposed to display data and show averages that we don't get with the free version. This is our first child, over paranoid doesn't describe it. We have a lactation consult Monday and his 2nd pediatric appointment in a few weeks. I would love to have detailed info to show both Dr.'s and make sure he's progressing as expected. Thanks for any help.\n\nSigned, concerned Dad.\n\nEdit: Was not expecting so much helpful feedback! My wife and I joined this group today, seems like we made the right choice! Thanks!\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+5 votes]\nUgh, I wish I had a more concrete answer for you but when baby is pretty content the premium (or plus, whichever one is cheaper) is relatively helpful. Right now our guy’s naps have been so erratic. He’s 5mo. I wouldn’t bother paying for the premium version until he’s a bit older. When they’re that young they are napping all the time and for like 20 minutes to two hours at a time. It’s unpredictable.\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+24 votes]\nWe used it to track poops, pee, feeding, sleep etc but our doctor pretty much said it was unnecessary.  I think we got a lot of value from it just in that it helped us feel more in control of an uncontrolled situation. Ex we tracked how much she ate daily, the pediatrician said as long as she's gaining weight the oz count doesn't matter. The one concrete thing I can said is at each pediatrician appointment they'd ask how many wet diapers does she have a day and we could refer to the app,but if we'd had a general idea that'd prob have been fine too.\nSo short answer, is the pro worth it? In objective value probably not but in subjective value if it makes you more confident and have some ease of mind then yea, it might be.  I believe the premium plan can take up to a month to provide you with a personalized sleep plan and I can guarantee 30 days later the problems you’re having will be different and the plan likely won’t be of much help!\n\n  [Saturday October 05 2024] [+2 votes]\n  That's my issue. That and it keeps asking me if she's transitioning to 2 naps and I say yes, but it's still only giving me sweet spot for 3 or 4 nap days\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+3 votes]\nWe used the free version of My Baby for our first child. It was more than enough for what we needed. We didn’t even bother with the app for our second lol.\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+79 votes]\nThe one that gives the sweet spot was helpful for me to learn my baby’s sleepy cues since they changed overnight and were less obvious. The upgrade that gives the personalized sleep plan was worthless. It was a generic sleep plan with tips that I could get off any website for free.\n\n  [Saturday October 05 2024] [+5 votes]\n  Agreed, the sweet spot predictor has been a game changer for naps and bed time. Highly recommend. We have a great sleeper who sleeps through the night since 6mo  and I’m sure having the sweet spot easy to know without any guesswork didn’t hurt.\n\n  [Saturday October 05 2024] [+22 votes]\n  Thirding this- sweet spot version is worth it just for tracking naps.\n  I swear it’s so accurate. I set up a notification 15 mins prior to baby’s naptime and she’s almost always asleep by the time the app suggests. We’re almost at 3 months and preparing to sleep train so it’s nice to have some structure around napping and sleeping. I think once she’s sleep trained I’ll stop paying for the app (but continue to use for tracking maybe)\n\n  [Saturday October 05 2024] [+2 votes]\n  Yepp this. We got the mid-tier that gave us sweet spot predictions and it’s been super useful (now 9 months old, been using it since birth).\n\n  [Saturday October 05 2024] [+7 votes]\n  Hard agree!! The sweet spot was a godsend the first 6-8 months. My LO would literally fall asleep within 10 minutes of when the app told us she would. We also completely cut out contact naps by 6 months, and she's been in her own crib from the beginning.  Around 8 months my LO would even start signing \"sleep\" when it was bedtime. She's been on a predictable sleep schedule ever since, and we still use it at 20 months just for tracking (free). She has a really good relationship with sleep.\n  The personalized sleep plan was absolutely garbage and a waste of money though.\n\n    [Thursday November 06 2025] [+2 votes]\n    Hi, I know this is an old post but just wondering how you were able to cut out the contact naps around 6 months old for your LO?\n\n      [Thursday November 06 2025] [+2 votes]\n      Hello! Once she would fall asleep, we would move her to the bassinet/crib, but I would stay right next to her. Then bit by bit I would distance myself physically. First I was next to her, then just in the room, then in a different room etc. Then once she was fine sleeping without contact that's when I started putting her to sleep on her own instead of initially falling asleep with contact.\nYES! The sweet spot for the wake/sleep windows is worth the price alone. So helpful! We also track nursing (not so much length that she ate but which side she last ate of off), diapers (I like to know how often she’s pooping especially as we get ready to start solids) and medication/vitamins.\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+2 votes]\nThe sweet spot with premium was scary on point lol. My son is sleep trained and I still check it occasionally to see if we’re on track with awake hours.\n\n  [Saturday October 05 2024] [+1 votes]\n  They charge for the sweet spot now? That used to be free.\n\n    [Saturday October 05 2024] [+1 votes]\n    I’m pretty sure! I was extremely sleep deprived about 11 months ago and was desperate for it and I’m pretty sure that was why! I know I paid because I’m about to cancel before it reups since he’s 1.\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+2 votes]\nOnly worth it if your baby’s sleep needs perfectly match what huckleberry thinks they should be. (Spoiler, they almost certainly won’t.) My baby’s always been on the low sleep needs side, so their wake window suggestions were almost always too short for us. The free trial of premium just sent me into a panic when I couldn’t get him to sleep when the app said he should be sleeping. Now I know homeboy just wasn’t tired yet.\nHuckleberry itself is pretty useful for me though. I’m still tracking sleep at 20 months, even though blessedly it’s mostly solid blue bars through the night. Whenever we hit a rough patch, I feel like looking at the data helps me troubleshoot (it’s almost always a reduction in sleep needs, and more time awake during the day gets everyone back to sleeping at night.)\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+9 votes]\nWhatever you do don’t buy the Premium one …. I was *so* desperate for sleep when baby was a newborn…. Everything in the sleep plan was completely generic, and they totally wrote off my specific concerns. (Baby was only sleeping in one hour chunks and totaling less than 12 hours a day — for weeks. They told me that was impossible, and he must be sleeping when I didn’t realize it. For the record, we used the Nara Baby app and I don’t remember paying for the premium version but we might’ve.\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+1 votes]\nSecond time mom here and I used the free with my first! Our pediatrician was on top of stuff since she was at risk of respiratory problems due to family history. It was nice to be able to keep track of stuff and pull up to see how many diapers in a day to say if it was less or more than normal.\nSome peds don’t care the oz amount as long as babe is gaining good. Ours noted both of our babies overate (first was formula second is breast/pumped bottle) due to the app and spit ups.\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+2 votes]\nI tried the huckleberry plus free trial, if I’m being honest I don’t think it makes that much of a difference and using the free version gives you enough data to start building a routine. I check the summary page and use the data that is given for free to get an idea of how much baby eats, sleeps, pees and poops. Although to be honest my biggest focus right now is sleep, I know she’s eating and peeing/pooping just fine\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+2 votes]\nWorth every cent!! I upgraded to premium (that includes a sleep plan) and it has been magical! I am one of those statistics where the sweet spot is literally spot on down to the minute.\nNow keep in mind, I didn’t use this app until my baby was 3mos and was really struggling with his night sleep. Naps were all over the place, poor baby was having 2hr wake windows and it was all around just bad. He is also on the low sleep needs so most sleeping books focused on 12hr nights that really messed up his overall sleep schedule.\nYes, I understand some people are fine without ever needing to track these things but for me it was crucial adjusting his schedule so he would get too much daytime sleep. You are able to submit your data every month for another reanalysis. The tips they gave me are incredibly insightful and it’s almost like sleep training at this point. I love that he’s slowly getting better and it’s peace of mind since we’re traveling a lot now and it auto adjust after a few days.\n  *I am a bot, and this action was performed automatically. Please contact the moderators of this subreddit if you have any questions or concerns.*\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+1 votes]\nWe used a similar app with our first for the first ~1.5 years. After that it sort went out of the window with toddlerhood.  Since the wife and I worked opposite schedules it was nice seeing how the day went at a glance but after a while we both kept forgetting to add food, diapers, naps etc and I stopped checking. Now with #2 we are using it again but will probably stop once they reach a year old or eating solids. The consistent tracking we did with the first doesn’t feel as important.\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+2 votes]\nSo we track sleep only and use the SweetSpot, which keeps us sane, although we use it as more of a guide. It gives us a pretty good idea of when she’ll probably want to go down for her next nap again, but obviously we watch for sleepy cues in case she’s having a day where she overly tired and goes down early. I like it because it helps me to plan what I’m doing throughout the day, since there is no way I would be able to keep track of the wake windows myself. I also like being able to take a look at past days and nights and see what her sleep patterns have been like at a glance. My memory wasn’t fantastic even BEFORE I had a baby, so this way I can look at older entries and see how many naps she’s typically taking per day, how often she’s getting that good 2 hour nap in, which days she’s sleeping later, what her wake windows are looking like. I like having the visual data to help make decisions about dropping naps and such.\nIt’s totally a personal thing though, and it’s currently working for us, so we’re using it. If at some point it starts not working for us, we’ll get rid of it.\nThat being said, we also found the personalized sleep plan pretty useless, but we also have a pretty easy baby (right now) when it comes to sleep, so I’m not sure if she really gave them much to work with to wow us with their insights.\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+2 votes]\nWe have used Huckleberry (not premium) pretty religiously - it's been great for us for tracking sleep and feeding (baby is 7 months old and we combo fed until a few weeks ago, so knowing the breast/bottle/pump info was helpful for us. For the first two weeks or so we tracked diapers too, but once we knew everything was normal there we stopped with that. When she's teething we also mark down any medicine she has taken so we remember how long it has been and no one accidentally overdoses her. We've found it very useful to track her naps and sleep. I activated the free trial of premium by accident when she was a few months old and we didn't find it worth it to keep. I should say, though, that our girl is a pretty good sleeper so we didn't feel the need to use their sweet-spot feature, which I know some folks love. If she was a fussier sleeper, I may have purchased premium. To your point about showing the detailed info to the doctor... I doubt they will ever ask for it unless something is wrong. \n  [Monday June 08 2026] [+1 votes]\n  Are you talking about plus or premium?\n\n    [Monday June 15 2026] [+1 votes]\n    Wondering too!\n\n      [Tuesday June 16 2026] [+1 votes]\n      I got plus shortly after I commented this and it does show the nap predictor (sweet spot) and if you look up discount codes online there’s one that gives you 40% off so I got plus annual for like 57.20 instead of 129 or something!! I recommend that because premium is sooo expensive and like other people said it’s kinda a useless upgrade when plus has everything you really need\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+4 votes]\nWe loved the free version until she was about 3 months old. Then as her daytime naps changed and she had legitimate wake windows we realized we were letting her get extremely overtired by accident. So we upgraded one level.  That has the \"sweet spot\" function that everyone is referencing.  Based on her age and previous sleep cycles it would give us a 15 minute heads up that she should be getting ready for a nap and we found that to be invaluable.\nAfter a month or so, I cancelled my subscription and stopped using the app entirely.  By that point I'd gotten into the groove of knowing her needs, routines, and general timeframes for hunger, poops, naps, etc.  so tracking wasn't giving me any useful information. It was just becoming a compulsion and fixation. I found myself forgoing sleep because I was so busy tracking the fact that my daughter had fallen asleep for 12 minutes.\nI think they're very useful to set reminders when you're learning to care for a newborn, and likely have extra utility for special needs babies.\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+2 votes]\nYou can use BabyTracker for free and it will show data and averages.  I used it for almost 2 years before Iet go\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+2 votes]\nPremium? No. The “personalized sleep plan” is a bunch of generic garbage you can find on any sleep consultant’s Instagram page. Pro? YES solely because of the SweetSpot feature. Of course, once your LO moves to a clock-based schedule, it will be less valuable, but we find that it takes the trouble out of tracking wake windows and keeps his daytime sleep on track!\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+4 votes]\nI think the first level of paying is enough with the predicted sleep which i find is almost to the minute right! I paid for the higher level for sleep analysis and it takes almost a month to get the report. By the time you get it the baby’s sleep has changed.\nDont pay for first two months as no routine until two months anyway. Then they’ll give you two weeks free when baby turns two months.\n\n  [Monday June 15 2026] [+2 votes]\n  This is info, my baby is almost 2 months\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+2 votes]\nI used the app for free and accidentally used the trial. First of all, use the app for free and then try the trial and see how it works for you.\nI LOVE data tracking and used it so much in the early weeks. Around 2-3 months, we stopped using it because we felt comfortable with her routine and sleep.\nI think the big part of premium is the sleep schedule. The sleep schedule didn’t make sense for us. She doesn’t do that many naps so the times didn’t sync up. It didn’t seem like anything fancy either, just a glorified timer?\nThe doctors don’t care about this information. They’ll ask you how many diapers they have on average (this is free), how much milk they get/how long they nurse (this is also free to track), and how the baby is sleeping (this is also free free free).\nI think the app just targets new parents. I’m sure someone found it helpful but I sure didn’t need it. The free features worked great for me.\n\n  [Saturday October 05 2024] [+1 votes]\n  I hadnt really thought about apps targeting new parents, that's a solid point! Thank you\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+2 votes]\nDidn’t help me at all, so we didn’t go for it. The free version is great for tracking everything else!\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+4 votes]\nYou will get a free trial when baby is the correct age that the sweet spot will start for you. Until then, no real point in paying unless you want the widget out to be able to get Siri to log the diaper for you.\nYou will know if it worth it by the end of the free trial (at 8 weeks adjusted I think). It was for us but not for other people I know\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+1 votes]\nAs others have said, the SweetSpot is worth paying for. It is really accurate and at 4 months it is definitely helping us navigate the 4-to-3 nap transition.\n\n[Saturday October 05 2024] [+1 votes]\nFor us, yes. We used the free version of the app to track everything at first (even how much I was pumping), then upgraded maybe 2 months in. It was super accurate helping us figure out wake windows, but i recognize that we may have just had a unicorn baby.\n\n[Sunday October 06 2024] [+1 votes]\nPremium as great for month 2-5. Then I felt pretty confident about naps and wake windows.\n\n[Sunday October 06 2024] [+1 votes]\nI downloaded it initially to track dirty diapers and get average time spent breast feeding and sleeping so I could accurately report to my gp for my LOs regular checkups. I paid for premium when my daughter started sleeping terribly at 11 weeks old. It was a bit of a waste of money. Honestly, while LO is under 3 months old you follow sleepy cues and after 3 months you try to follow more of a wake window style schedule. Good luck, and remember: the days are long, but the weeks, months, years are short.\n\n[Sunday October 06 2024] [+1 votes]\nFor us, the sweet spot upgrade was worth it (the middle tier). The personalized plan we've never tried mostly because lots of people say it's useless.\n\n[Sunday October 06 2024] [+1 votes]\nI use Nara Baby instead. Completely free and you can track your own stuff on it too! (We only use for feeding and diapers)\n\n[Sunday October 06 2024] [+1 votes]\nI used just the free version for 9 months. The 2 week trial of sweet spot got us through the 4 month regression and I had a handle on my baby's sleep after that.\n\n[Sunday October 06 2024] [+1 votes]\nThe ux sucks in my opinion.  What to expect is a better app with more features.\n\n[Sunday October 06 2024] [+1 votes]\nYes. It helped us sooooo much. We all get more sleep and love it\n\n[Friday June 06 2025] [+1 votes]\nSuch a good purchase, and couldn’t recommend enough- literally super accurate and helped our baby not go crazy getting overtired especially at night time.\n\n[Saturday November 15 2025] [+1 votes]\nWe tried Huckleberry too - free version is fine for tracking, but the premium predictions felt pretty generic and didn't match our baby's actual patterns.\nA friend recommended Betteroo and we've had better luck with it since it personalizes based on both baby's needs AND what we can realistically handle as exhausted parents. Most apps just optimize for the baby without considering we're running on fumes.\n\n[Friday January 09 2026] [+1 votes]\nI used Huckleberry Premium for a bit. The tracking and Sweet Spot were helpful, but the higher-tier features felt pretty generic and not really worth paying for long term.\nRecently I’ve been using newparent.ai instead. It’s less about tracking every detail and more about helping you figure out what to do next based on what’s actually happening. The free version has been enough for me so far, so it might be worth a try if you’re on the fence about subscriptions.\n\n[Wednesday April 15 2026] [+1 votes]\nfollowing--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHuckleberry: Baby Tracker Negative Reviews & Comments (https://appsupports.co/1169136078/huckleberry-baby-tracker/negative-reviews)\nciteturn73search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; \n✓ App is so glitchy It started out great, then about 6 months in the app became very glitchy, logs don’t save the first time so I have to enter them multiple times, doing this several times an hour all day long has become extremely frustrating, to the point where I just skip logging things. Customer support were no help at all so my problem persists. Definitely not renewing my membership.. MegTurtle Version: 0.9.243\n\n✓ Plus not worth it I like the tracking features that are free but I bought plus to try and do not like it. The schedule tracker has too long wake windows and the manual mode only lets you adjust wake windows by 15, 30 or 45 mins. Even then I tried to adjust it and it didn’t work. Definitely not going to renew my subscription to plus.. Ariqryuoo Version: 0.9.238\n\n✓ Wish I hadn’t paid for premium I’ve amended my review based on the developers response however I’m still wishing I hadn’t paid for the premium version of this product. I’ve received our personalised sleep plan and whilst it’s quite comprehensive in terms of the amount of information, it does all seem fairly generic guidance and it feels a bit like certain sections have been added depending on the issues noted (eg reflux,teething) with the child’s name changed and with quite general information. I would say it’s tailored rather than personalised as there’s no specific commentary on my child’s sleep or why the schedule has been recommended. It also says to follow wake windows rather than the schedule at her age (5 months).If I’d known this I probably wouldn’t have paid. We’re only on day three of implementing the plan and so far daytime sleep has been a lot worse but I’ve reached out to Huckleberry so will see what they say.. Apple-M-30 Version: 0.9.160\n\n✓ Huge survey to then ask to pay App overall seems okay, but after spending 10 minutes doing something like a “sleep survey” it then only at the END tells me to upgrade and pay more to unlock. This should be stated CLEARLY at the beginning. Not everyone a) can afford the extra expense and b) wants to sit through a massive questionnaire just to be told they need to then pay. This is extremely predatory and banking on sunk cost fallacies.. RyanWin Version: v0.9.296\n\n✓ This app is wonderful however SO buggy I started using this app when my son was 2 days old and have loved it- it takes so much pressure off trying to remember which side I fed off last and how long he’s been awake for. However as soon as I uploaded to a Premium membership to be able to use the Apple Watch feature the app has been logging me out everytime I close the app, log anything via my watch, a nappy or even in the middle of a feed. It’s frustrating having to log in every single time it decides to crash and log me out. I’ve considered cancelling my Premium membership as this encounter only started when I upgraded.. Annaaa17 Version: v0.9.248\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBaby Sleep Answers (https://www.babysleepanswers.com/baby)\nciteturn73search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; \n“The course is easy to understand and it was perfectly fit for our needs.”\n\nImage\n\nAndreea Corbu\n\nOctober, 2022\n\n“I strongly believe that Andrea is a genius. The course is easy to understand and it was perfectly fit for our needs. After only one night my baby is sleeping much better and I am confident about what is next. Good job and thank you!”\n\n“So if you’re still wondering whether to do it or not, DO IT. Your future self will thank you.”\n\nImage\n\nNancy Contarino\n\nJune, 2022\n\n“The first time I came across Baby Sleep Answers was on a random Instagram post late one night while I was rocking my baby back to sleep for, what felt like, the hundredth time searching for “baby sleep tips” and I was hooked. At that point I used Andrea’s sleep tips and posts on her page but never actually bought a sleep plan. BUT for my second son, there was no way I was going to put myself through the same late night heartache AND not knowing how to deal with a toddler PLUS an infant that won’t sleep, so I finally bought her plan and WOW, I wish I had done it way sooner with my first son because even though the posts on her page helped me SO much, buying an actual plan and following it was the best thing I did the second time around. #### Frequently Asked Questions\n\nWill this course continue to be updated? And will I have access to future versions?\n\nYes! We are continuously improving and updating our course and once you get it: it’s yours forever! In fact, if there is something you don’t see in the course that you would like to, just email us and we’ll add it!\n\nI’m very busy, how long does it take to get through the course?\n\nTotally depends on you! You could finish it in just a couple of hours and come back to it whenever you need, or you can take it slow, watch all the videos at your pace as you need them, and of course, always come back to them.\n\nCan I use FSA or HSA to pay?\n\nYES! Andrea is a Certified Holistic Drugless Practitioner and as one you can pay for all of our courses and consultations with your FSA or HSA! First Contact your FSA administrator and tell them you’d like to have sleep consultation services covered. They may ask you to ask your pediatrician for a letter of medical necessity, this is easy! Then If you use FSA you may have to prepay and then ask us for a receipt to submit to your FSA administrator. “...it’s like Andrea knows our baby and what he’s about to do!”\n\nImage\n\nStefanie Wind\n\nMay 2023\n\n“This course has been extremely helpful for our baby’s sleep! We bought the baby package and love how it includes multiple options and grows with our baby. We had purchased another course and corresponding consultation with a different sleep consultant, but Baby Sleep Answers was more helpful AND less expensive. Our baby still has some hiccups with his sleep but I have always been able to refer to the course pdf and find helpful tips--it’s like Andrea knows our baby and what he’s about to do! I have already recommended this course to my friends who are expecting and I hope they buy it sooner than I did. :) Thanks, Andrea and Joe!!!”\n\n“When I got the plan, I was blown away with all the options in mind that were tailored to my family.”\n\nImage\n\nJessica Frattare\n\nJuly, 2022\n\n“I bought a sleep plan for my 7 month old who had such a hard time sleeping, and doing it independently. Andrea recommended the course for my baby’s age and when purchasing, it asked a bunch of questions so it could be tailored to my child, as well as my schedule, our nursing schedule, and my expectations. When I got the plan, I was blown away with all the options in mind that were tailored to my family. After 3 nights, my baby was sleeping 8 hours straight and falling asleep within 15 minutes. It’s been 2 weeks and she now sleeps about 11-12 hours each night and 1-2 hours for both of her naps, in her crib independently. We are SO thankful for this sleep plan. THANK YOU, ANDREA!”\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n‎Huckleberry: Baby Tracker - Ratings & Reviews - App Store (https://apps.apple.com/ca/app/huckleberry-baby-tracker/id1169136078?platform=iphone&see-all=reviews)\nciteturn73search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; \n# Another money hungry company\n\n    1.     2.     3.     4.     5. 2025-03-30\n\nMissgirlicious\n\nI initially paid $76 for Huckleberry’s SweetSpot feature, expecting full access to the tools I needed to track my baby’s sleep. Now, with their latest update, they’re asking for even more money just to use the widget!!! A feature that was previously included. It feels like a blatant cash grab, especially for parents who already paid for a premium service!! The app itself is helpful, but this constant paywalling of basic functionality is frustrating and unfair to loyal users. If you’re considering paying for Huckleberry, be aware that they may keep locking features behind new subscriptions. I regret investing in this when there are free alternatives out there. So disgusted by your company!\n\nDeveloper Response 2025-04-02\n\nThank you for your feedback, but you should be able to access widgets if you have plus membership. We have not changed any of the Plus features you had before in the newest update. If you can't access widgets, that might be a different issue, so please reach out to us at support@huckleberry-labs.com so that we can get it fixed for you.\n\n  * ### Amazing\n\n    1.     2.     3.     4.     5. Sep 11\n\nBosse607\n\nThis app is so helpful! Making things easier for a first time mom and dad. It’s nice to not have to try and remember how many diapers he’s had or when he ate last when you’re exhausted!\n\n# Amazing\n\n    1.     2.     3.     4.     5. Sep 11\n\nBosse607\n\nThis app is so helpful! Making things easier for a first time mom and dad. It’s nice to not have to try and remember how many diapers he’s had or when he ate last when you’re exhausted!\n\n  * ### Charging $150 for sweet spot\n\n    1.     2.     3.     4.     5. 2021-06-04\n\nKdp91!\n\nThis used to be a great app, I used to recommend it to every expectant/new parent I’d meet and mommy groups I was in. I’ve used it for almost 2 years and was planning on using it for baby coming in Dec. the sweet spot indicator was free and that’s what made this app stand out from all the rest, and if you were struggling to get baby to sleep, they have dxx dob m the option to pay for a personalized plan which is great if you need it. The sweet spot took the guess work out of nap/bed time (wasn’t always correct but still close enough that I’d be able to read my baby and see if he needed to go down earlier or slightly later than what it was recommending). Now after almost 2 years of use they decided to start charging parents for the sweet spot indicator..$150 a year! Are they nuts? Oh but wait if you purchase it now, you can get it half off for $75 🙄 if it was $10 maybe I’d consider it. But I can literally just go online and figure out what baby’s wake window is based on age and then just calculate it myself, or I can also just read my baby’s cues for crying out loud. It really isn’t that hard to figure out, not enough at least to pay $150. I understand it costs money to make an app and keep it up but by offering the sweet spot for free, at least more parents went on it and many were probably willing to pay for the personalized plan. Now, I won’t be recommending it to anyone unless they make it free again because it’s literally no different than any other free sleep tracking app, except with other apps you won’t be constantly hassled to pay for their services!\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHuckleberry: Baby & Child App Reviews & Download - Medical App Rankings! (https://appsrankings.com/app/1169136078/huckleberry-baby-child)\nciteturn73search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: last month; \nGentlespirit1999990\n\nPremium Annual Membership. I feel cheated for paying for this app. I paid for their highest membership - the premium plan for an entire year. When I first started with this app, it was great because the best thing you were getting for the money you paid was the custom plans and advice by sleep professionals after you summit your data for analysis. However the amount of time required by them before getting back to you with advice/your plan has now increased to 12 business days. 12 business days is almost 3 weeks! For goodness sakes, we are talking of baby sleep issues. By the time 3 weeks roll around, the issues I asked help for are probably entrenched and gotten so worse because almost a month has gone by or the problem has moved into a different problem because again, it’s been almost 3 weeks! The turnaround time definitely needs to be shorter. It’s unfair. When I started with the app, the turn around time was shorter. When I purchased the app, I was sold it by the ads showing how you should pay all this money for this high plan so you can get advice. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBaby Registry Review – Expert Feedback to Perfect Your Registry - Baby Chick (https://www.baby-chick.com/services/registry-review/)\nciteturn73search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; \n\n# Registry Review:\nPerfecting Your Baby Registry\n\nImage: Registry\n\nAlready have your baby registry set up but want to ensure it’s absolutely perfect? Nina’s Registry Review service is here to help you feel confident and prepared.\nWith her years of expertise and deep knowledge of baby products, Nina will provide a thorough evaluation of your registry, identifying any gaps, recommending must-haves you might have missed, and offering alternatives to better suit your family’s unique lifestyle and needs.\nSometimes, all it takes is a fresh perspective to turn a good registry into a great one!\n\nImage: Background Review\n\nWhat's Included:\n\n  * Nina's Complete Registry Form: A personalized questionnaire to better understand your family's needs and preferences.\n  * 60-Minute Phone Consultation: A one-on-one discussion with Nina to discuss your current registry and address your concerns so that she can tailor her r